      "id": "airway",
      "title": "Prepare for safer intubation",
      "subtitle": "Prepare the physiology, people and rescue plan—not only the laryngoscope.",
      "minutes": 22,
      "phase": "Respiratory support",
      "outcomes": [
        "Identify technical and physiological preparation priorities.",
        "Describe airway confirmation, depth checks and reassessment.",
        "Plan post-intubation care and a supported handover."
      ],
      "roleNotes": {
        "ug": "Name the physiological risks and explain how the team prepares; drug prescribing is outside this course.",
        "nurse": "Prepare checked equipment, monitoring and suction, confirm team roles and assist drug double-checks under local policy.",
        "picu": "Anticipate peri-intubation instability, confirm monitoring and circuit readiness, and track fixation/depth after movement.",
        "pg": "Lead a primary/rescue strategy, optimize physiology with senior help, assign stop points and plan post-intubation support."
      },
      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "airway-prepare",
          "title": "Prepare people and physiology",
          "paragraphs": [
            "An apparently straightforward laryngoscopy can still be dangerous in a severely hypoxaemic, shocked or acidotic child. Call experienced help early; review oxygenation, perfusion and the consequences of interrupting breathing. The safest preparation addresses both anatomy and physiology.",
            "Assign an airway operator, assistant, medication/monitoring roles and a clear leader. Check suction, oxygen, ventilation equipment, tube/fixation supplies, primary and rescue devices, and whether the team can use them. An equipment list without an executable rescue plan is incomplete."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "aha-als"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "airway-plan",
          "title": "Make the primary and rescue plans explicit",
          "paragraphs": [
            "State the intended first approach, alternatives, limits on repeated attempts and when to pause, reoxygenate and change the plan. Preoxygenation builds oxygen reserve; it does not make an unlimited period without effective ventilation safe.",
            "Before medication, use the best available verified weight and the local prescribing/double-check process. This module does not provide induction drug doses. A learner should recognize the need for checking and appropriate supervision rather than infer dosing authority from an online result."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "aha-als"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "airway-confirm",
          "title": "Confirm tracheal placement and evaluate depth",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Use sustained exhaled CO₂ detection/waveform capnography with clinical assessment when available. Examine chest movement and air entry, assess oxygenation, record tube depth and secure fixation. Tube misting or colour improvement alone is insufficient confirmation.",
            "Markedly reduced pulmonary blood flow or cardiac arrest can make exhaled CO₂ detection unreliable. An absent signal requires urgent evaluation; do not assume a single explanation. A positive CO₂ signal also does not prove the tube is at the correct depth: a mainstem tube can still produce CO₂."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "aha-als"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "airway-after",
          "title": "The procedure continues after tube placement",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Prepare ventilator connection, continued oxygenation/ventilation monitoring, appropriate sedation/analgesia under the clinical plan and haemodynamic support. Check delivered values and alarms rather than only the set mode.",
            "After moving the child, reassess fixation, depth, chest movement, bilateral air entry and gas exchange. A new asymmetry or deterioration calls for immediate patient–tube–circuit assessment. Do not assume that the earlier confirmation remains sufficient after transport or repositioning."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "aha-als",
            "palicc"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "airway-case",
          "title": "Worked example: a technically easy, physiologically risky airway",
          "paragraphs": [
            "A child with severe pneumonia is exhausted and poorly perfused. The team predicts an easy view but recognizes high risk of desaturation and hypotension during the transition to invasive support. They organize experienced help, physiological stabilization, suction, monitoring and a backup ventilation plan before attempting intubation.",
            "After successful placement, the team checks CO₂ and bilateral air entry, secures and records depth, then reassesses circulation and delivered ventilation. The question is not just “is the tube in?” but “is the child safely supported now?”"
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "aha-als",
            "course"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "airway-depth",
          "title": "A useful stop point",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Repeated attempts without re-optimization can worsen hypoxaemia and airway trauma. A shared stop point prompts the team to pause, oxygenate, call expertise and change the approach rather than repeat the same failure."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "good",
          "sourceIds": [
            "aha-als"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "airway-enrich",
          "title": "Rehearse the first minute after intubation",
          "paragraphs": [
            "On a manikin, ask each team member to state what they check after placement and after movement. Identify who responds to hypotension, missing CO₂ or an unexpected pressure alarm."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "nice",
          "sourceIds": [
            "course"
          ]
        }
      ],
