      "id": "ventilation",
      "title": "Read the ventilator through the child",
      "subtitle": "Connect delivered volumes, pressures, gas exchange and circulation before changing a setting.",
      "minutes": 26,
      "phase": "Advanced practice",
      "visual": "waveform",
      "outcomes": [
        "Distinguish controls for oxygenation and CO₂ clearance.",
        "Interpret delivered volume and basic pressure/flow patterns.",
        "Explain a bounded lung-protective strategy and reassessment plan."
      ],
      "roleNotes": {
        "ug": "Explain what each variable represents and why a setting is not a patient outcome.",
        "nurse": "Read back prescribed settings, check delivered values/alarms and report concerning patient trends.",
        "picu": "Track delivered tidal volume, pressure, expiratory flow, synchrony and circulation; escalate concerning changes within the agreed plan.",
        "pg": "Integrate mechanics and gas exchange before adjusting ventilation; justify a lung-protective strategy and recognize its exceptions."
      },
      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "ventilation-controls",
          "title": "Separate the goals of the controls",
          "paragraphs": [
            "FiO₂ and airway-pressure/recruitment strategy influence oxygenation. Effective alveolar ventilation influences CO₂ clearance. Minute ventilation is tidal volume multiplied by respiratory rate, but useful ventilation also depends on dead space and the volume actually delivered.",
            "Example: 120 mL × 20 breaths/min is 2.4 L/min. The same displayed minute ventilation can be less effective when dead space is larger or leaks are substantial. Increasing rate without allowing adequate expiration can worsen air trapping in obstruction."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "palicc",
            "course"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ventilation-delivered",
          "title": "Inspect what reaches the patient",
          "paragraphs": [
            "After connection, assess the child, tube, circuit, exhaled tidal volume, pressures, gas exchange, circulation and alarms. A set tidal volume does not prove the delivered volume matches it, especially with leak and circuit effects.",
            "Look at trends and the clinical response after every change. Pressure-targeted and volume-targeted strategies allow different variables to vary; understand the chosen mode’s behavior and limits. Alarm settings must match the patient and intended support, not be silenced by default."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "palicc",
            "aha-als"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ventilation-mechanics",
          "title": "Resistance, compliance and time",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Peak pressure includes resistive and elastic components. With appropriate static measurement conditions, a higher peak pressure while plateau pressure is unchanged suggests increased resistance. If plateau also rises, consider reduced respiratory-system compliance among the causes. Coughing, effort and measurement conditions can confound this interpretation.",
            "An expiratory flow curve that does not return to zero before the next breath suggests incomplete exhalation. In obstructive disease, this raises concern for dynamic hyperinflation/auto-PEEP. Assess the child, timing and cause; simply increasing rate may make it worse."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "palicc"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ventilation-protect",
          "title": "Lung protection is a set of constraints",
          "paragraphs": [
            "For PARDS, PALICC-2 suggests physiological tidal volumes around 6–8 mL/kg, reducing below 6 when needed to respect pressure limits, with caution below 4. Its general plateau-pressure suggestion is at most 28 cm H₂O, with specified chest-wall exceptions; driving pressure is suggested at most 15 cm H₂O under static conditions. These are qualified recommendations, not a universal initial prescription.",
            "Use the lesser of predicted or actual body weight for the guideline’s size-normalized measures. Titrate PEEP with oxygenation, delivery, haemodynamics and compliance together. A larger volume just to normalize a gas may worsen injury."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "palicc"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ventilation-gas",
          "title": "Worked example: accept a bounded trade-off",
          "paragraphs": [
            "A fictional PARDS case has high pressures while the team tries to normalize PaCO₂. PALICC-2 permits selected permissive hypercapnia to a lower pH limit of 7.20 to remain within protective pressure/volume constraints. This does not mean ignoring severe acidosis or circulation.",
            "Important exceptions include intracranial hypertension, severe pulmonary hypertension, selected congenital heart lesions, haemodynamic instability and significant ventricular dysfunction. Review the whole patient and the full guideline/local plan. Contrast obstruction: there, allowing time for exhalation may be central to avoiding air trapping."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "must",
          "sourceIds": [
            "palicc",
            "course"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ventilation-depth",
          "title": "Oxygen exposure also needs a goal",
          "paragraphs": [
            "PALICC-2 suggests SpO₂ 92–97% in mild/moderate PARDS, with qualified lower saturation acceptance in severe disease after PEEP optimization and closer delivery monitoring. This is a disease-specific example. It should not be substituted for all pediatric or cardiac targets."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "good",
          "sourceIds": [
            "palicc"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ventilation-enrich",
          "title": "A static measurement is a clinical condition",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Before interpreting a peak–plateau difference, ask whether the child is passive enough and the inspiratory hold/measurement appropriate. Numbers from different conditions may not be directly comparable. Discuss this with the PICU team during supervised waveform review."
          ],
          "points": [],
          "depth": "nice",
          "sourceIds": [
            "palicc"
          ]
        }
      ],
